legend:
You step into your first data structures class, where you are learning about binary search. You heard the professor yapping about why binary search works in O(logn). But you want to see if you can find a better bound.

Given a sorted array a of size n and an integer k, define f(a,k,l,r) as the result of the following code:

function f(a, k, l, r):
   if a does not contain k:
      return 0
   mid = floor((l+r) / 2)
   if a[mid]==k:
      return 1
   else if a[mid]<k:
      return 1+f(a, k, mid+1, r)
   else:
      return 1+f(a, k, l, mid-1)


You are given two integers n and m. Define an array a good if:

|a|=n (there are n elements in a).
1≤a1≤a2≤…≤an≤m (the array is nondecreasing, bounded by 1 below and bounded by m above).
You are interested in finding the sum of f(a,1,1,n)+f(a,2,1,n)+…+f(a,m,1,n) over all good arrays a. Since this answer may be huge, output the answer modulo 676767677. Note that 676767677 is a prime number.
